title: Keep storage-reading aliasers out of `AllowExplicitUnpaidExecutionFrom`

doc:
  - audience: Runtime Dev
    description: |
      `AllowExplicitUnpaidExecutionFrom<T, Aliasers>` emulates origin-altering instructions before
      `UnpaidExecution`, calling `Aliasers::contains` for every `AliasOrigin` it sees. Barriers run
      before any payment is taken, so `Aliasers` must be pure computation: passing a filter that
      reads storage (such as `pallet_xcm::AuthorizedAliasers`) lets any incoming message trigger
      several unmetered storage reads.

      The barrier's doc comment previously told runtime authors to set `Aliasers` to the same type as
      `xcm_executor::Config::Aliasers`. It now says to use a cheap, computation-only *subset* of it,
      and explains that aliases only allowed by the excluded (expensive) filters can still buy
      execution via the paid barrier. No logic changed in the barrier.

      The system parachain runtimes are restructured to demonstrate the pattern: `TrustedAliasers` is
      split into a `CheapTrustedAliasers` part (computation only) plus `AuthorizedAliasers<Runtime>`.
      The executor keeps using the full `TrustedAliasers`, while the barriers get
      `CheapTrustedAliasers`. `AuthorizedAliasers` stays last in the tuple, so the invariant that
      cheaper filters are evaluated first is preserved. As a side effect, cheap `AliasOrigin` +
      `UnpaidExecution` flows are now accepted by these chains' barriers, matching the executor.

